The product listing uses the **`productSearch`** query from the product-discovery drop-in (`scripts/__dropins__/storefront-product-discovery/api.js`, sent as a GET to Catalog Service). Here it is with its fragments expanded:

```graphql
query productSearch(
  $phrase: String!
  $pageSize: Int
  $currentPage: Int = 1
  $filter: [SearchClauseInput!]
  $sort: [ProductSearchSortInput!]
  $context: QueryContextInput
) {
  attributeMetadata {
    sortable { label attribute numeric }
    filterableInSearch { label attribute numeric }
  }
  productSearch(
    phrase: $phrase
    page_size: $pageSize
    current_page: $currentPage
    filter: $filter
    sort: $sort
    context: $context
  ) {
    total_count
    items { ...ProductView }
    facets { ...Facet }
    page_info { current_page page_size total_pages }
  }
}
```

**Per product** (`ProductView` fragment, from `fragments.js` in the same folder):
- `sku`, `name`, `inStock`, `url`, `urlKey`, `__typename`
- `attributes` (name, label, value, roles) and `images` (label, url, roles)
- Simple products: `price` — final and regular amount (value + currency)
- Complex products: `priceRange` (min/max, each with final and regular) plus `options` with swatch values
- `highlights` (attribute, value, matched_words) for search-term matches

**Per facet** (`Facet` fragment): `title`, `attribute`, and `buckets` — category (name, count, path), scalar (count), range (from, to, count), or stats (min, max).

Two things worth knowing:

- The response is normalized by a client-side transformer before blocks see it, so block code works with camelCase fields (`pageInfo.currentPage`, `totalItems`) even though the wire query uses snake_case.
- This is the drop-in's vendored code — you can't edit the query directly, but you can extend what's returned via the drop-in's model transformer config, and the query goes wherever `get_commerce_endpoints` says the storefront points (Catalog Service or mesh).

The drop-ins expose **109 CSS custom properties** (design tokens), all scoped under the `.dropin-design` class. Five categories:

| Category | Count | Examples |
|---|---|---|
| Colors | 32 | `--color-brand-500` (#454545), neutrals 50–900, positive/warning/alert/informational sets, button states |
| Typography | 35 | `--type-base-font-family`, display 1–3, headline 1–2, body 1–2 (default/strong/emphasized), buttons, captions |
| Spacing | 13 | `--spacing-xxsmall` (4px) up to `--spacing-xxhuge` (192px) |
| Shapes | 14 | border radii 1–3, border widths 1–4, shadows 1–3, icon strokes |
| Grid | 15 | columns/margins/gutters for 5 responsive breakpoints |

**To see current values in your project:** open the site, then in DevTools inspect any element with the `dropin-design` class — the Computed/Styles panel shows every `--color-*`, `--type-*`, etc. with its live value. Or run this in the console:

```js
const el = document.querySelector('.dropin-design');
const s = getComputedStyle(el);
console.log(s.getPropertyValue('--color-brand-500'));
```

**Where they're set in the code:** in an EDS storefront the overrides live in the styles folder (typically `styles/styles.css`, in a `.dropin-design { ... }` rule). Anything not overridden there falls back to the defaults listed above.

**To retheme**, you just override the variables:

```css
.dropin-design {
  --color-brand-500: #0066cc;
  --color-brand-600: #0052a3;
  --type-base-font-family: 'My Brand Font', sans-serif;
}
```

